THE GOAL

Instant-green tree (hypothetical, non-standard): no amber warning before
green. A box at the car watches the tree and sends the launch command
(transbrake release, or clutch/throttle on cars without a transbrake).
The tree is L = 15 ft = 4.572 m away.

THE LAUNCH POINT (defined so nobody has to argue about it)
"When the car launches" is fuzzy: switch contact? solenoid opening?
clutch starting to slip? To avoid that, the clock starts at the one event
the box itself controls and a scope can see: the instant the box's own
launch output switches (the electrical edge at its output connector).
Everything after that edge - solenoid or valve release, clutch, converter,
tire roll, beam - is lumped into t_car.

Definitions (times in seconds):
  t_green = the timing system's green signal (what the RT clock uses)
  t_bulb  = (green lamp visibly on) - t_green
            incandescent ~ 0.03 s, LED close to 0
  t_box   = (box output switches) - (box sees the green lamp)
  t_car   = (stage beam clears) - (box output switches)
  RT      = (stage beam clears) - t_green
  RT = 0.000: beam clears exactly at green. Negative RT = red light.

For RT = 0 the box's output must switch at t_green - t_car, i.e. t_car
BEFORE the green signal, and t_car + t_bulb before the lamp it is
watching visibly lights.

Working estimates by class (Portatree's published typical rollouts,
which are timed from the driver's launch command to the beam - car only).
The bulb lag is NOT in these numbers; it is the separate t_bulb term, so
nothing is subtracted for an LED tree:

  Class                     t_car         Advance needed   Advance as multiple
                                          before green     of light time (L/c)
  Top Fuel dragster (est.)  ~ .10 s       ~ .10 s          6.6 million
  Super Comp dragster       .18 - .22 s   .18 - .22 s      11.8 - 14.4 million
  Pro Stock car             .24 - .28 s   .24 - .28 s      15.7 - 18.4 million
  Super Stocker (~3200 lb)  .30 - .34 s   .30 - .34 s      19.7 - 22.3 million
  Door cars, rule of thumb: t_car ~ (weight in lb, with driver) / 10,000 s
  (Sportsman/door-car rule only. It does NOT fit nitro dragsters: a
  ~2,300 lb Top Fuel car would come out ~.23 s, about double the row above.)

  Top Fuel row: a rounded physics estimate, not a published figure.
  ~11.5 in of tire roll to clear the beam at ~5 g gives ~0.11 s, less if
  deep-staged; real cars take a few hundredths of a second to build up
  that force, so 0.10 s is a best-case floor, not a typical value. Like
  every row, it is car-only (no bulb delay).

  light time across the lane:  L / c = 4.572 / 299792458 = 15.25 ns
  (Examples below use the best case, t_car = 0.10 s.)

A bookkeeping number people sometimes quote: v_eff = L / (-advance)
(Top Fuel -45.7 m/s, Super Stock -13.4 m/s). Careful: its size SHRINKS
as the job gets HARDER. Judge difficulty by the advance column only.

The size of the advance is nothing. The sign is everything: this is not a
request for a fast signal, it is a request to receive a signal before it
is sent. Heavier classes make it worse - they need MORE advance.

Repeatability and red lights: a fixed lead cancels the AVERAGE t_car, not
its run-to-run spread (sigma_car). Displayed .000 means RT within about
+/- 0.0005 s; to land there 99% of the time needs sigma_car under about
0.0002 s. And since any negative RT is a red light
(an outright loss), even a box with perfect future information would aim
slightly LATE: aiming RT = +3 x sigma_car red-lights about 1 run in 740.
Example: sigma_car = 0.002 s -> aim RT = +0.006 s. Realistic target:
small positive RT, no reds. The class ranges above (about +/- 0.02 s) are
differences BETWEEN cars, not one car's run-to-run spread, which has to
be measured on the car.

FOUR THINGS THAT ARE NOT THE SAME
  1. Superluminal signal  - arrives faster than light, but AFTER it is sent.
  2. Backward signal      - a chosen message arrives BEFORE it is sent.
  3. Predictive correlation - data line up with the future, but nobody can
                              choose what they say (no message).
  4. Prediction           - the future is already fixed by present data.
Only 2 (or 4) gives the box what it needs.

WHY STANDARD PHYSICS PREDICTS ZERO

1. Fronts travel at c. Group velocity can exceed c or go negative,
     vg = dw/dk = c / ng,   ng = n(w) + w * dn/dw
   (Wang et al. 2000 measured ng = -310 in caesium vapour), but that moves
   the PEAK of a smooth pulse, not new information. A switch closing or a
   lamp turning on is a front, and fronts arrive at L / c, never earlier.
2. Kramers-Kronig relations tie n(w) to absorption/gain at all
   frequencies: steep dispersion is never free and never acausal.
3. A standing wave is two travelling waves:
     sin(kx) * cos(wt) = (1/2) * [ sin(kx - wt) + sin(kx + wt) ]
   A change at one mirror reaches the other no sooner than L / c, and a
   cavity must fill (energy ring-down ~ L / (c * (1 - R))), so it is
   slower, not faster.
4. Standard quantum mechanics is linear and no-signalling: one side's
   measurement choice never changes the other side's local statistics.
5. The box's required output (0.10 s before the green) and the tree's green
   are TIMELIKE separated
   (c x 0.10 s = 29,979 km >> 4.572 m). No change of reference frame can
   put the box event after the green. One-way FTL gives 0 s of warning;
   the most any one-way trick can save over 15 ft is L / c = 15.25 ns.
   That holds under any single preferred "now" as well, provided every link
   runs forward in its time; only FTL that is instant in two different
   frames can loop back (shape i; frame options in the wave-law note,
   section 10).

Conclusion: the old Method A (fast light in IR-pumped smoke, ng = 0.1)
and old Method B (mirror cavity) are standard causal physics, so they
predict a null result. Removed. Below: every framework we found that does
NOT predict null, each given a status:
  CONTROL  - works, but reads the present, not the future
  OPEN     - consistent in logic; no evidence for it yet
  BOUNDED  - experiments already push it far below a usable size
  CAPPED   - even if real, too small to supply 0.10 s

THE ONLY THREE SHAPES A NON-NULL BOX CAN TAKE
  (i)   a causal loop: FTL out to a relay moving relative to you and FTL
        back (the "tachyonic antitelephone"), or a closed timelike curve.
        Antitelephone geometry: box and tree at rest together; a relay a
        distance D away moves at speed v along the line between them. The
        box sends a signal that is instantaneous in ITS frame; the relay
        replies instantly in the RELAY's frame. The reply arrives early by
          dt = v * D / c^2   ->   D = c * dt / (v / c)
        (the Lorentz factor gamma cancels in this setup).
        For 0.10 s:  v ~ c: D = 29,979 km.
                     v = 0.1c: D = 299,792 km (most of the way to the Moon).
        These are minimums: with any finite signal speed u > c on each leg
        the advance is smaller and D must be larger.
        
        (((Frame option P' in the wave-law note, section 10. Planetary relay
        (rough, circular orbits): at orbital speeds
        (~1e-4 c) D must be AU-scale. Best-case advance, a_E * a_p * |n_p - n_E| / c^2
        (orbit radii a, orbital rates n), with the relay on
          Venus ~0.02 s   Mars ~0.035 s   Jupiter ~0.24 s
          Saturn ~0.46 s  Neptune ~1.5 s
        so Jupiter or farther covers the class table, but only when the
        planets' positions and motion line up (other times it comes back
        late, and zero when the two planets line up with the Sun). It needs
        FTL links that are instant in EACH world's own
        frame - the two-frame case the wave-law note flags as its open
        limit (its section 8) - and a working relay out therhebe.)))

  (ii)  direct retrocausation: a future condition changes a present
        measurable.
  (iii) the green is already fixed by present data the box can read.
Check the shape before the physics.

CANDIDATE 0 - THE GREEN IS ALREADY DECIDED (shape iii, no new physics)
How "unpredictable" the green really is depends on what decides it:
  a. Human starter's button: humans have rhythms and habits, so timing is
     partly predictable statistically, never exactly. Ordinary forward
     causality - the decision forms in the starter before the press.
  b. Automatic random start: only as random as its generator. A
     pseudo-random generator is deterministic once its seed/state is
     fixed; a hardware or quantum noise source is unpredictable in
     practice.
  c. Pre-arranged or rigged timing (an agreed signal, a repeatable
     non-random pattern): not physics, it is cheating. Out of scope.
"Is anything truly random?" (deterministic chaos, superdeterminism) does
not change the box's problem. Even in a fully deterministic universe the
box still needs the deciding information to physically reach it before
green; determinism without a readout predicts nothing.
In cases a and b, the deciding information exists before the lamp lights -
in the controller, the starter, or electrical/RF leakage from the tree. A
box reading it is reading the present early, not the future.
  Test: log every accessible signal (photodiode on the green, logic
  analyser, RF pickup) for 100+ passes; cross-correlate with real green.
  Confirm: some present signal predicts green to < 0.001 s, >= t_car
  ahead. Falsify (for that tree): none does.
  Status: CONTROL. The only candidate known to work anywhere. Using it to
  launch is prohibited in sanctioned racing; in this section it is the
  control that every physics candidate must be isolated from.

CANDIDATE 1 - NON-EQUILIBRIUM PILOT WAVE (Valentini)
Breaks: the Born rule as a law. In de Broglie-Bohm theory it is an
equilibrium state:
  equilibrium:      rho = |psi|^2       (no signalling)
  non-equilibrium:  rho != |psi|^2      (signalling allowed)
Catch 1: the signal is instantaneous in the theory's preferred frame -
arrival AT the moment of sending, not 0.10 s before. With only one
preferred frame, a loop (shape i) can't send it back earlier either: 0 s.
Catch 2: no non-equilibrium matter has ever been found; relaxation runs
toward rho = |psi|^2, so it can't be made in a lab. Proposed sources are
relic particles from the early universe (CMB searches).
Status: OPEN - not refuted in logic, no evidence, no lab source, and even
if found it gives 0 s of advance warning (see Catch 1).

CANDIDATE 2 - NONLINEAR QUANTUM MECHANICS (two different theories)
Breaks: linearity.
 2a. Weinberg-type (1989): Gisin (1990) and Polchinski (1991) showed this
     kind of nonlinearity lets one end of an entangled pair read the other
     end's measurement choice - instantaneous signalling, loopable via
     shape (i).
     Bound: Bollinger et al. (1989), 9Be+ nuclear spin: nonlinear part
     < 4 x 10^-27 of binding energy per nucleon.
     Status: BOUNDED.
 2b. Kaplan-Rajendran causal framework (2022): nonlinearity built so it
     never signals faster than light. Even if real it gives 0 s of advance
     warning - it is listed only because it is the version being tested now.
     Bound: Melnychuk et al. (2025), cryogenic RF: electromagnetic
     nonlinearity |epsilon| <~ 1.15 x 10^-12.
     Status: BOUNDED, and causal by design.
2a is the cleanest route to a real signal, but experiments show any
such signal would be virtually indistinguishable from a coin flip,
even over a huge number of trials.

CANDIDATE 3 - ADVANCED WAVES (Wheeler-Feynman; Cramer)
Breaks: "retarded waves only." Fields are half retarded + half advanced:
  F = (1/2) * (F_retarded + F_advanced)
With a perfect future absorber the advanced parts cancel exactly. If
absorption is incomplete, an emitter might depend on its future absorber -
true retrocausation. BUT an ordinary advanced wave on the direct path
from the tree reaches the box at most L / c = 15.25 ns before green.
Ceiling on the direct path: 15 ns, not 0.10 s. (An advanced wave tied to a
distant absorber could in principle lead by L_path / c; 0.10 s needs a
path of ~30,000 km, and no absorber effect has been shown at any range.)
Evidence: Partridge (1973), microwave source power constant to ~1 part in
10^8 (null). Pegg's reanalysis (1973): a static absorber must give null
anyway, so Partridge's setup could not settle it - a time-varying absorber
is needed (Heron & Pegg 1974). Bajlo (2017, not peer reviewed) claims
advanced pulses ~14.3 ns early over 4.3 m - exactly D / c for that 4.3 m,
so even if real it fits the direct-path ceiling. Cramer's UW entangled-
photon experiment (begun 2007) reported no signal; Cramer & Herbert's 2014
analysis explains why: quantum interference cancels the would-be signal.
Cheapest real test: Heron-Pegg style - microwave/laser source
behind an isolator; downstream shutter/absorber whose state is chosen by a
quantum random number generator AFTER each emission window; lock-in
measurement of source drive power vs future absorber state, analysis fixed
in advance. Confirm: power tracks future state > 5 sigma. Falsify: null at
the rig's sensitivity. Cost: a hobby rig (~$200-500) reaches roughly
1 part in 10^4-10^5; matching Partridge's 10^8 needs lab-grade sources and
detectors ($2,000+). Main false positive: ordinary back-reflection (SWR) - the
isolator and a matched dummy-load control are the experiment.
Status: CAPPED (direct path) and no evidence; the expected result is null.

CANDIDATE 4 - CLOSED TIMELIKE CURVES / WORMHOLES
General relativity allows them on paper (Goedel, Tipler, Gott, wormholes
with one mouth moved relativistically). Quantum models: Deutsch (1991),
Lloyd et al. (2011, post-selected), Ji-Wilde-Lloyd (2026, capacity of a
noisy post-selected CTC channel - mathematics, not a device).
If one existed at the strip, 0.10 s would be trivial. The cost:
  needed at a 1 m throat:  rho ~ -c^4 / (8 * pi * G * r^2) ~ -5 x 10^42 J/m^3
  best real negative energy (Casimir, plates 1 um apart):
                           rho = -pi^2 * hbar * c / (720 * a^4) ~ -4 x 10^-4 J/m^3
  at plates 10 nm apart: ~ -4 x 10^4 J/m^3 (gap still ~38 orders)
  gap: ~46 orders of magnitude at 1 um, before Ford-Roman quantum
  inequalities and Hawking's chronology protection (1992) are even invoked. Gott's version
  cannot be built from normal matter in an open universe (Carroll, Farhi,
  Guth & Olum 1994).
All lab "CTC experiments" are simulations; post-selected ones learn which
runs "worked" only afterwards, through ordinary channels.
Status: OPEN on paper in general relativity; as engineering, 38-46
orders of magnitude short on negative energy.

CHECKED AND REJECTED
- Lorentz violation / tachyon particles: one-way FTL gives 0 s of warning
  to a stationary box; photon-speed limits put the scale of a linear
  energy-dependent shift beyond 10 x the Planck energy, and of a quadratic
  one beyond about 6e-8 of it (LHAASO, GRB 221009A, 2024; 95% lower limits
  within those two models).
- Tachyonic FIELDS (m^2 < 0): standard QFT; means instability, not speed;
  signals still never exceed c.
- Objective collapse (GRW / CSL / Diosi-Penrose): random by design, no
  control, no message. Parameter-free Diosi-Penrose ruled out underground
  at Gran Sasso (Donadi et al. 2021).
- Two-state vector / weak values / delayed-choice eraser: real
  future-dependence in post-selected data; no control, no message.
- Superdeterminism: predicts correlations, offers no channel or test.
- A real instant (Coulomb-gauge) potential with a receiver coupling
  (the wave-law note's model): faster than light only in one preferred
  frame, so never early; saves at most 15 ns, and at the strip it would
  only sense the tree's own circuit switching - Candidate 0 by another
  route.
- Hidden finite-speed influences behind entanglement: Bancal et al. (2012)
  showed any finite-speed model would allow FTL signalling; Salart et al.
  (2008) bound such an influence at > 10,000 c (if Earth moves < c/1000 in
  the preferred frame), and Cocciaro, Faetti & Fronzoni (2018) at about
  5 million c for a CMB-like frame (loopholes still open). Only models that
  are exactly instant in one frame escape these bounds (Candidate 1, the
  wave-law note), and those give 0 s.
- Fast light, superluminal tunneling (Nimtz), Scharnhorst effect
  (dc/c ~ 1e-36): peak reshaping or unmeasurably small; all <= 15 ns.
- Presentiment / precognition: pre-registered replications at chance
  (Ritchie, Wiseman & French 2012); no controllable channel.

WHAT A WORKING BOX WOULD HAVE TO DO (requirements for any candidate)
R1 Shape: a loop (i) or direct retrocausation (ii) reaching at least
   t_car + t_box into the future: 0.10-0.34 s by class, or the measured
   t_car of the real car. Present-data tricks (iii) don't count.
R2 Precision: the green time it delivers must be right to sigma <~ 0.0002 s
   for a displayed .000 99% of the time; ~0.002 s still gives a useful
   "small positive RT, no reds" box.
R3 Information: pinning a green that can fall anywhere in a 1-2.5 s window
   to 0.001-0.0002 s takes about 10-14 bits per launch, delivered early.
R4 Reliability (yes/no "green now?" calls, detection strength d'):
     d' ~ 4.65 -> 99% right: about 1 miss per 100 launches.
     d' ~ 6.18 -> 99.9% right: about 1 miss per 1,000 launches.
   For scale: a box that only just passes the random-bit test below at
   55% has d' ~ 0.25 - about 18x too weak to race with. (Rule of thumb:
   an effect of size e takes ~1/e^2 trials to tell apart from a coin
   flip, and each launch gets exactly one trial.)
R5 Isolation: must work against a tree run by a hardware/quantum random
   generator with every present-data path blocked (Candidate 0 control).
R6 Practical: car-mounted, battery-powered, no lab-scale equipment.
No candidate above meets R1 with known physics; a new result that does is
exactly what step 6 of WHAT TO DO NEXT watches for.

THE RANDOM-BIT TEST (how to check any claimed box, ~$120-150)
Protocol: the box must COMMIT a guess (GREEN / NOT GREEN) and latch it in
hardware at t = -0.10 s (or -t_car for the class being tested). Only
then does an independent random generator (avalanche-noise or quantum
source, checked for bias) pick the bit at t = 0. Separate battery
supplies, shielded enclosures, no shared wiring,
nothing discarded, analysis written down before the first trial.
Control run: event station powered off (catches leakage).
Statistics (chance = 50%):
  N = 2,500 trials:   55.0% correct = 5 sigma
  N = 10,000 trials:  52.5% correct = 5 sigma
  N = 62,500 trials:  51.0% correct = 5 sigma
Physics predicts 50%. Any repeatable 5-sigma excess, replicated on a second
build, would be the discovery. This also exposes post-selection tricks:
the box can't change its guess after the bit exists.

DEMONSTRATIONS OF WHY THE OLD METHODS FAIL (optional, cheap)
- Negative-group-delay op-amp circuit (Nakanishi, Sugiyama & Kitano 2002,
  seconds-scale design): smooth pulse peak exits early; an abrupt step
  never does. (~$50 + any scope)
- 15 ft of RG-58 (velocity factor 0.66) + NanoVNA: one-way delay 23.11 ns,
  open-stub resonance 10.82 MHz, mode spacing 21.64 MHz - the standing
  wave obeys L/v, it doesn't erase it. (~$115)

THE PRACTICAL FLOOR
Without future information the box can only react:
  RT = t_bulb + t_box + t_car
  t_bulb: ~0.03 s incandescent, near 0 LED.
  t_box (photodiode + fast comparator + hardware input capture to the
         output edge): well under 0.001 s.
  t_car: the dominant term (class table above); includes the solenoid or
         clutch actuator's own response - measure it.
So the best causal result is RT ~ +0.10 s (Top Fuel, est.) to ~ +0.34 s
(Super Stock), plus ~0.03 s on an incandescent tree - better than a
typical driver-launched car on instant green (racers report ~0.5 s), but
never .000.
On a standard .400 pro tree, the ambers ARE the advance warning - 0.400 s
of it, delivered by light, for free - which is why .000 is possible there
and not here.
Rules: sanctioning bodies (NHRA, IHRA) prohibit any device that senses
the tree to trigger the launch, in every class; delay boxes, where allowed,
must be started by the driver. Test-and-tune / private use only - check
the current rulebook for the class.

FINAL RESULT
Not proven impossible - but no established physics, and no proposed
physics that has survived testing, supplies the 0.10-0.34 s of advance
information (depending on class) needed at a drag strip. One-way FTL
gives 0 s; ordinary advanced waves are capped at 15 ns on the direct
path; loops need a relay ~30,000 km away at near light speed (or as
far as Jupiter at planetary speeds, with two-frame FTL), or a time
machine 38-46 orders of magnitude beyond available negative energy. The
one workable "candidate" reads the present, not the future. What a real
box would need is spelled out in the requirements (R1-R6); the random-bit
test is the cheap way to check anyone's claim against them.

WHAT TO DO NEXT (in order)
1. Measure the car. Log (box output edge) -> (stage beam clears) over
   20+ launches with a practice beam and a hardware-timestamping logger.
   Record mean t_car, sigma_car and worst case. This one number set
   replaces the class estimates and sets the aim-late margin.
2. Build and characterise the reacting box (photodiode + fast comparator +
   microcontroller input capture -> launch output). Measure t_box on the
   bench, and t_bulb on both an LED and an incandescent tree.
3. Run the random-bit test against the box (or any device someone claims
   works). Publish the raw log, including the powered-off control run.
   Expected result: 50%. That null is a real, citable result.
4. Optional demonstrations for the doc: the negative-group-delay circuit
   and the 15 ft coax/NanoVNA test (both under ~$120).
5. Optional science side-quest: a hobby Heron-Pegg absorber test. It
   cannot help the box (15 ns ceiling), but it is the one open retro-
   causal question a home lab can actually poke at.
6. Reopen this section only if one of these happens, independently
   replicated and peer reviewed:
     - a measured deviation from the Born rule (Candidate 1),
     - a nonzero nonlinearity in quantum mechanics (Candidate 2),
     - a confirmed advanced-wave effect (Candidate 3),
     - any device passing the random-bit test above chance.
   If one does, re-score that candidate and rerun the random-bit test.
7. Meanwhile, the gains that actually exist are ordinary ones: lower and
   more repeatable t_car (staging depth, front tire diameter, launch
   setup), a fast low-jitter box, and an LED tree - within the rules of
   the class being raced.

